<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sturmabteilung</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as SA, Brownshirts, Storm Division</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sturmabteilung, or SA, was the original party militia of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, called the Brownshirts after the colour of its uniform. Formed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> rose to party leadership in 1921, it protected Nazi rallies, broke up opponents' meetings, fought the paramilitary units of the Communist and Social Democratic parties and intimidated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, trade unionists and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. By the end of 1933 it numbered more than 3 million men. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/night-of-the-long-knives">Night of the Long Knives</a> in 1934 its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-rohm">Ernst Röhm</a> was arrested and executed, and the SS superseded it. The SA took part in the Kristallnacht pogrom of November 1938 and was disbanded and outlawed by the Allied Control Council in 1945.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Sturmabteilung was first applied to the assault troops of Imperial Germany in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, who used infiltration tactics in small squads. The Nazi group developed from the ex-soldiers and beer-hall brawlers who protected meetings of the German Workers' Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. On 3 August 1921 Hitler redefined it as the party's Gymnastic and Sports Division, and after a brawl at the Hofbräuhaus on 4 November 1921, which the Nazis called the Saalschlacht, the group was officially known as the Sturmabteilung.</p><p>The brown shirts came from a shipment originally meant for German colonial troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, which Gerhard Roßbach bought in 1921 for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> unit. After the failed Beer Hall Putsch Ernst Röhm helped create the Frontbann as a legal alternative while the SA was banned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The SA fought street battles with members of the Communist Party, and it recruited from the unemployed in the economic crisis. Hitler took supreme command in September 1930 after the Stennes revolt and asked Röhm to return as chief of staff. The SA numbered about 400,000 by January 1932 and about 2,000,000 when Hitler took power in January 1933, twenty times the size of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichswehr">Reichswehr</a>.</p><p>Röhm wished to absorb the army, limited to 100,000 men by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, into the SA, which alarmed the generals and other Nazi leaders. On 30 June 1934, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/night-of-the-long-knives">Night of the Long Knives</a>, Hitler arrived at Bad Wiessee with SS units and arrested Röhm and other SA leaders. Most estimates place the number killed at between 150 and 200, though some say up to 1,000. Röhm was shot by two SS officers on 1 July after refusing to kill himself. The SA lost almost all its influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In November 1938, after the assassination of the diplomat Ernst vom Rath, SA members shattered the glass of about 7,500 Jewish shops and businesses in the Kristallnacht pogrom and damaged or destroyed about 200 synagogues; more than 30,000 Jewish men were arrested and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. In January 1939 the SA became a training school for the armed forces, and most of its members were lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> after September 1939.</p><p>It was organised into regional Gruppen, each of Brigaden made up of regiment-sized Standarten, with its command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. The Allied Control Council disbanded and outlawed the SA on 10 October 1945 under Control Council Law No. 2.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sturmabteilung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its words were written in 1929 by Horst Wessel, a Sturmführer of the party's paramilitary <b>Sturmabteilung</b> (SA, the &quot;Brownshirts&quot;) in the Friedrichshain district of Berlin, and were published in September 1929 in Der Angriff, the Berlin party newspaper owned and run by Joseph Goebbels.</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li></ul></section>
